course: dictionary
lesson: 6992
title: Par: equal, normal, and golf
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Par is a small word with a few different jobs. In golf, par is the number of strokes (hits) a good player should need to finish a hole. Scores are counted against par: 2 under par means 2 strokes fewer than par.
explain: Most of the time you will meet par in idioms, which are phrases with their own meaning. «The new library is on a par with the best in the city» means it is just as good as the best. «I'm feeling a bit below par today» means I am not quite as well as usual.
explain: To check a meaning, swap in a simple phrase such as "as good as" or "not as well as usual". If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Par means the normal or expected level, the standard you measure things against.
fact: «On a par with» means equal to or as good as.
fact: «Up to par» means as good as it should be.
fact: «Below par» or «under par» means not as good, or not as well as usual.
fact: «Par for the course» means normal or expected, usually for something annoying.
exercise: «The new library is on a par with the best in the city.» What does on a par with mean? | Far worse than the others | Closed for repairs | *Equal to, as good as | Next to the sea
exercise: Mum says, «I'm feeling a bit below par, so I'll rest.» What does below par mean? | Hungry for lunch | *Not feeling as well as usual | Feeling very happy | Ready for a race
exercise: In golf, Sam says, «I finished two under par.» What does that mean? | Two strokes more than expected | Two holes won | *Two strokes fewer than expected | She played two games
test: «My homework is up to par, so I'm pleased.» What does up to par mean? | Very late | Lost | *As good as it should be | Too long
test: Dad says, «Losing my keys again? That's par for the course.» What does par for the course mean? | *Normal and expected, especially something annoying | Something rare and special | A sports game with prizes | A brand new golf course
exam: Which phrase means "equal to, as good as"? | Under par | Below par | *On a par with | Par for the course
